(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 55-year-old man is having a split-night polysomnogram. The first 2 hours of sleep show significant obstructive sleep apnea. What happens during the rest of the night?","choices":["Bilevel positive airway pressure titration","Adaptive servo-ventilation titration","Untreated recording until morning","Continuous positive airway pressure titration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a split-night polysomnogram the first 2 hours of sleep are recorded to determine severity, and if obstructive sleep apnea is significant, the rest of the night is a continuous positive airway pressure titration that finds the exact pressure needed to keep the airway open. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 26, 33"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman's polysomnogram shows events with no airflow. In each, there is no respiratory effort during the first part of the event, and effort resumes during the latter part while airflow is still absent. How are these events classified?","choices":["Obstructive apnea","Central apnea","Hypopnea","Mixed apnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A mixed apnea has no airflow, with a lack of respiratory effort during the first part of the event and resumption of effort during the latter part. Her events begin like central apneas and end like obstructive ones, which is exactly the mixed pattern. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea has no teeth and wears full dentures. He cannot tolerate his continuous positive airway pressure mask and asks about a mouthpiece instead. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy","Oral appliance","Radiofrequency ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in patients who have no teeth (or in severe obstructive sleep apnea). He is edentulous, so the mouthpiece he asks about is the option to avoid, even though he cannot tolerate his mask. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman with a body mass index of 44 kg/m2 has daytime hypersomnolence, loud snoring and dyspnea on exertion. Awake on room air, her arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 54 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Her chest radiograph, thyroid function tests, pulmonary function tests and toxicology screen show no other cause of hypoventilation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Narcolepsy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Obstructive sleep apnea alone","Central sleep apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a diagnosis of exclusion made when obesity (body mass index above 30) and awake alveolar hypoventilation (arterial carbon dioxide pressure above 45 mm Hg) are present and alternative causes of hypercapnia are excluded. Her 44 kg/m2, 54 mm Hg and negative workup meet all three. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 30, 24, 10"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea declines treatment and asks how the condition affects his heart. Which risk can be raised as much as 6 times in untreated disease?","choices":["Sudden cardiac death during exercise","Hypertension","Driving accidents","Sudden cardiac death at night"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries a 3- to 6-fold risk of sudden cardiac death at night, alongside high mortality from heart failure. The timing is the point: the excess risk falls during sleep, when the apneas occur. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 15"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has trouble falling asleep and asks for a sleeping pill. Which option should be avoided?","choices":["Positional therapy with a sleep shirt","A sedative sleeping pill","Diet and exercise for weight loss","Referral to a sleep physician"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modification is to avoid alcohol and other sedatives. A sedative sleeping pill would add a risk factor to his untreated apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 12"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has central sleep apnea after a stroke. His heart function is normal, he takes no opioids and he lives at sea level. A trial of continuous positive airway pressure has failed. What is the next therapy to consider?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation","Reducing opioid medications","Autotitrating positive airway pressure","Bilevel positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In central sleep apnea, continuous positive airway pressure is considered first, and if it fails, adaptive servo-ventilation is next: it monitors breathing patterns in real time and delivers and adjusts pressure as needed. With normal heart function he lacks the reduced ejection fraction that makes it unsafe. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 12, 33"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with a body mass index of 38 kg/m2 has obstructive sleep apnea. Which additional finding, with no other cause found for it, should prompt screening for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["Awake room-air saturation of 93%","Overnight lowest saturation of 86%","A serum bicarbonate in the normal range","Loud snoring with witnessed apneas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obese individuals, with or without obstructive sleep apnea, are screened when they have an unexplained awake room-air oxygen saturation of 94% or less or an overnight lowest under 80%, unexplained exertional dyspnea, signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure, facial plethora or a raised bicarbonate. 93% awake qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 31, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman snores loudly. She feels rested during the day, no one has seen her stop breathing, and she is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 36 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 14 inches. Using the STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) questionnaire, which result fits her?","choices":["Score 1, low risk","Score 2, low risk","Score 2, high risk","Score 3, intermediate risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed 0 + pressure 0 + body mass index 36 (more than 35) 1 + age 35 0 + neck 14 inches 0 + female 0 = 2, the low-risk band (0 to 2). The body mass index rule needs 2 or more yes answers to the first 4 questions, and she has 1, so she stays low risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had a stroke last year. His wife reports loud snoring and pauses in his breathing during sleep, and a home sleep apnea kit has been ordered. What is the best next step?","choices":["Change the order to a polysomnogram","Proceed with the home kit as ordered","Start positive airway pressure now","Prescribe modafinil for sleepiness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stroke is one of the conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Stroke is also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, and at-home testing assesses obstructive sleep apnea and is not sufficient for central apnea, so the order should change. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11, 12, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has severe obstructive sleep apnea on polysomnogram. She finds her mask uncomfortable and asks whether an oral appliance would work as well. What should she be told?","choices":["It is not effective in severe disease","It is the most effective option","It works if she sleeps on her back","It equals the mask if worn nightly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An oral appliance is not effective in severe obstructive sleep apnea or in patients without teeth. Her disease is severe, so the appliance is not a substitute; continuous positive airway pressure remains the most effective treatment for her. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea uses continuous positive airway pressure nightly but still has daytime sleepiness. He also has coronary artery disease, cirrhosis and generalized anxiety disorder. Which treatment calls for caution because of his other conditions?","choices":["Positional therapy","Diet and exercise for weight loss","Modafinil","A nasal steroid spray"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stimulants such as modafinil or armodafinil are used with caution in patients with cardiovascular, hepatic or psychiatric conditions, and he has all three: coronary artery disease, cirrhosis and an anxiety disorder. Nervousness and anxiety are also listed side effects. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 15"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness, and his primary care clinician suspects obstructive sleep apnea. Which referral is part of his care?","choices":["Cardiologist","Sleep physician","Bariatric surgeon","Maxillofacial surgeon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician, together with proper counseling. His snoring, witnessed apneas and sleepiness make the diagnosis likely, and confirming it takes a sleep study. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 15, 13, 18"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and obstructive sleep apnea has failed a weight loss program and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure. Which second-line therapy is described as very effective?","choices":["Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery for weight loss","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Autotitrating positive airway pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For patients who fail or do not tolerate first-line therapy, second-line options include tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation, which is very effective, and bariatric surgery, which may be helpful. She has failed weight loss and cannot tolerate positive airway pressure, so she qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has a body mass index of 36 kg/m2. He asks what he can do himself, in addition to using his positive airway pressure device. Which risk modification fits him?","choices":["A nightly sedative to deepen sleep","An evening glass of wine to relax","Diet and exercise for weight loss","Sleeping on his back each night"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity heads the risk factors for obstructive sleep apnea, and diet and exercise for weight loss is a named risk modification, together with avoiding alcohol and other sedatives. Bariatric surgery for weight loss may also be helpful. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 12, 13, 15"},{"q":"A 38-year-old long-haul truck driver has untreated obstructive sleep apnea with marked daytime sleepiness and is reluctant to start treatment. What should he be told about his rate of driving accidents compared with people without the disorder?","choices":["1.5 times higher","2.5 times higher","3 times higher","3 to 6 times higher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries 2.5 times the rate of driving accidents, because daytime sleepiness increases motor vehicle crashes. For a man who drives for a living with marked sleepiness, that risk is a reason for counseling and treatment. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 15, 20"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man was diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea on a sleep study at another center and now needs the pressure on his new device set. Which study is used?","choices":["Overnight diagnostic polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit","Pressure titration study","Split-night polysomnogram"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A positive airway pressure titration is used when the diagnosis of sleep-disordered breathing is already known; it finds the exact air pressure needed to keep his airway open. His diagnosis is established, so a diagnostic study is not repeated first. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has heart failure with reduced ejection fraction and central sleep apnea. His heart failure therapy has been optimized, and continuous positive airway pressure has failed. Which therapy should be avoided because of his heart failure?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation","Continuous positive airway pressure","Optimized heart failure therapy","Repeat polysomnography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive servo-ventilation is the usual step after continuous positive airway pressure fails, but it is not used for every patient, especially in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: with poor left ventricular function, reducing preload can decrease cardiac output. Cheyne-Stokes breathing can also be protective. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who takes long-term opioid medications has central sleep apnea confirmed on polysomnography. He has no heart failure and lives at sea level. Which step treats the underlying cause?","choices":["Reduce the opioids","Start adaptive servo-ventilation","Optimize heart failure therapy","Add a stimulant for sleepiness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is treated first by addressing the underlying cause, and when the patient takes opioid medications, the step is to reduce the opioids. After that, continuous positive airway pressure is considered, and adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24, 11"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea drinks no alcohol. An abdominal ultrasound done for another reason shows hepatic steatosis, and he has no edema or jugular venous distension. Which comorbidity of his sleep disorder fits this finding?","choices":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Alcohol-related liver disease","Liver congestion from right heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have a 2 to 3 times higher prevalence of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease. Hepatic steatosis in a man who drinks no alcohol, with no signs of right heart failure, fits that comorbidity. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 20, 31"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 has loud snoring and daytime sleepiness. His awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure is 49 mm Hg (normal 35-45). Which sleep study should be avoided in his evaluation?","choices":["Pressure titration once diagnosed","Home sleep apnea kit","Polysomnography with carbon dioxide monitoring","In-laboratory polysomnogram"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In patients with awake hypoventilation a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit, and home kits assess obstructive sleep apnea without other sleep disorders. His awake carbon dioxide of 49 mm Hg is awake hypoventilation, and the gold standard for suspected obesity hypoventilation is polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 32"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has a polysomnogram as part of a research study. He has no sleepiness, fatigue or insomnia, and no one has noted snoring or pauses in his breathing. The study shows 7 predominantly obstructive events per hour. How should this result be interpreted?","choices":["Diagnostic of obstructive sleep apnea","Diagnostic of central sleep apnea","Needs positive airway pressure therapy","Criteria for diagnosis are not met"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Without symptoms, the diagnosis needs 15 or more predominantly obstructive events per hour; 5 or more counts only with a symptom such as sleepiness, fatigue, insomnia, choking or snoring noted by an observer. He has 7 events and no symptoms, so neither criterion is met. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 26"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness. During 6 hours of sleep his polysomnogram records 48 apneas, 42 hypopneas and 36 respiratory effort-related arousals, predominantly obstructive. What is his apnea-hypopnea index?","choices":["7 events per hour","8 events per hour","15 events per hour","21 events per hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The apnea-hypopnea index is (apneas + hypopneas) divided by total sleep time in hours: (48 + 42) / 6 = 90 / 6 = 15 events per hour. Respiratory effort-related arousals belong to the respiratory disturbance index, not to this one. At 15 or more predominantly obstructive events, the diagnosis is confirmed. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness. His insurer suggests a home sleep apnea kit. Which test should be used instead?","choices":["Home sleep apnea kit at a later date","Positive airway pressure titration","Thyroid-stimulating hormone level","In-laboratory polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits suit uncomplicated suspected obstructive sleep apnea, but in significant cardiopulmonary disease a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit. His heart failure and chronic lung disease are that comorbidity, and heart failure is also a risk factor for central apnea, which a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with obstructive sleep apnea has seasonal allergic rhinitis, and the nasal congestion makes her positive airway pressure mask hard to use. Which treatment addresses her congestion?","choices":["Modafinil each morning","A sedative at bedtime","Positional sleep shirt","A nasal steroid spray"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nasal steroids are used in patients with obstructive sleep apnea for nasal congestion and inflammation from allergic rhinitis. Treating her congestion targets the problem that is getting in the way of her positive airway pressure therapy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 6, 15"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a body mass index of 43 kg/m2, hypersomnolence and a raised serum bicarbonate is being evaluated for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Which test is the gold standard for her?","choices":["Home sleep apnea test","Daytime arterial blood gas alone","Sleep study with carbon dioxide monitoring","Sleep study with oxygen saturation alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Following carbon dioxide through the night captures the hypoventilation of the syndrome alongside the obstructive events that coexist in about 90% of patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 30, 8"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man is seen for evaluation before elective knee surgery and has no known sleep disorder. His wife says he snores loudly enough to be heard through closed doors and has seen him stop breathing during sleep. He denies daytime tiredness. He is treated for high blood pressure. His body mass index is 33 kg/m2 and his neck (shirt collar) measures 16.5 inches. What is his STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 4","Score of 5","Score of 6","Score of 7"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 0 + observed apnea 1 + treated pressure 1 + body mass index 33 (not more than 35) 0 + age 56 (older than 50) 1 + neck 16.5 inches (16 or larger) 1 + male 1 = 6. A score of 5 to 8 places him in the high-risk group for obstructive sleep apnea before his surgery. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with obesity hypoventilation syndrome asks what most often causes death in people with his condition. What should he be told?","choices":["Hypercapnic respiratory failure","Motor vehicle crashes","Complications of diabetes","Cardiovascular disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The leading cause of death in obesity hypoventilation syndrome is cardiovascular disease. Untreated, the syndrome tends to progress, and many patients develop pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure; mortality and morbidity are high. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 30, 34, 18"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man started modafinil for residual daytime sleepiness from obstructive sleep apnea. Two weeks later he develops a widespread rash and feels unwell. Which adverse effect of the drug best fits?","choices":["Worsening of his sleep apnea","Headache from the drug","Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Nervousness and anxiety"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Listed side effects of modafinil and armodafinil include headaches, nervousness or anxiety, dizziness, nausea, and dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivities. A widespread rash with feeling unwell after starting the drug fits a dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivity reaction. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman who takes long-term opioid medications for chronic pain has frequent awakenings, nonrestorative sleep and daytime sleepiness, with little snoring. Her polysomnogram shows repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Obstructive sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Narcolepsy","Central sleep apnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Repetitive apneas in the absence of thoracic-abdominal excursion are the polysomnogram finding of central sleep apnea: airflow stops because respiratory effort stops. Opioid medications are a listed risk factor, and frequent awakenings, nonrestorative sleep and sleepiness with little snoring fit. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 26, 10"}]};
